{"translation": "For in all the passages quoted he evidently mentions [645] both sleeplessness and coma. And again, writing of another patient in the same book, he says: 'In this man throughout, from the time that I too knew him, the bowel was disturbed, urine thin, black; he was comatose, sleepless.' So it is plainly evident that with him the name of coma signifies neither sleep nor torpor (kataphora). For this reason, I suppose, some of the exegetes say that the comatose are named from 'revelling' (kōmazein). For revellers are awake, and the comatose are awake too. And, by Zeus, just as revellers do not nod down at all, so neither do those in a coma, and especially not with their eyes. For their eyes are moist and red and unsteady and sunken. But in both the whole body is unsteady in its movements and lacking in control. For this reason, they say, Hippocrates himself is also at a loss about the whole syndrome, whether one should call these patients phrenitics or something else.", "renderings": [{"target_no": 8, "surface": "κώματος", "english": "coma", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 21, "surface": "τουτέῳ", "english": "In this man", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 30, "surface": "ταραχώδης,", "english": "disturbed", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 31, "surface": "οὖρα", "english": "urine", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 32, "surface": "λεπτὰ", "english": "thin", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 34, "surface": "κωματώδης,", "english": "comatose", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 50, "surface": "κώματος", "english": "coma", "uncertain": "n"}, {"target_no": 128, "surface": "φρενιτικοὺς", "english": "phrenitics", "uncertain": "n"}], "uncertain_greek": ["ἐνδεδυμένους", "κατανεύουσιν", "ἀστηρίκτους", "καταφορὰν", "ἐξ οὗ καὶ ἐγὼ οἶδα"]}
